# A call that ENDS a handle's life may not be handed one the caller borrowed
# (panel 145, defect 031; author decision 2026-09-13).
#
# **The defect.** A `record Holder { cell: ptr }`, a copy, `free` through the
# copy inside a function that takes its `Holder` WITHOUT `@`, then a write
# through the original's own field: build exit 0, run exit 0, both prints
# reached, and `heap-use-after-free, WRITE of size 8` under `--sanitize` on
# Darwin and on Linux. Nothing in the language refused any line of it.
#
# **What this rule is, and what it deliberately is not.** Panel 139 measured
# that a rule keyed on *reaches C through a `ptr`* cannot be written from the
# headers at all — `sqlite3_column_count` is not const-qualified, so a mutating
# step and a pure read have the identical C signature, and such a rule would
# refuse 13 of 17 functions in the shipped SQLite binding, four of which mutate
# nothing. So the signal comes from the binding author or from nowhere: one
# word, `consumes`, written once per consuming function.
#
# The rule it buys is **local** and that is the whole of its cost. A plain
# parameter is BORROWED (Part 5): the caller keeps the value and sees it again
# after the call. Handing such a value to a call that frees it is a promise the
# signature cannot keep, and it is decided by the argument's place root and the
# enclosing function's parameter list — two facts on the page, no flow analysis
# (`check/leasing.hero` states that the checker has none).
#
# **What it does NOT close, said here rather than discovered later.** Repaired
# to `closed(@h: Holder)`, the program compiles again and `h` is copied out, so
# a COPY made before the call still holds the freed address and ASan still
# fires. That shape — one copy freeing what another holds — is an affine
# handle, which is core by §1.7 and the Part 6 borrow-checker row's territory,
# and design.md Part 8 wart 20 is where the language states the cost it keeps.
# This rule refuses the program that was filed; the class above it is refused by
# transitivity and written down rather than patched.

use ast
use source
use token
use diag
use resolved
use check/state
use check/leasing

# Every consuming parameter of a declaration, by position. Absent for anything
# that is not a function, and empty for a function with no marked parameter,
# which is almost all of them.
function consuming_positions(decl: ast.Decl) -> [i64]
    out: [i64] @ []

    match decl.kind
        .function_decl fd =>
            at: i64 @ 0

            for p in fd.value.params
                if p.consumes
                    out @ out.push(at)
                at @ at + 1
        .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => out @ out

    return out

# Is this expression's place rooted in a parameter the enclosing function
# BORROWED — a parameter written without `@`?
#
# `leasing.place_root` walks a field path or an index down to the name it
# stands on, which is exactly the question here: `h.cell` is `h`. The resolver
# then says what that name IS, so the rule never has to carry the enclosing
# function's parameter list around: a `.param` local that is not `mutable` is a
# parameter written without `@`, and nothing else in the language is.
function borrowed_root(a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, at: i64) -> i64?
    root = leasing.place_root(a, at: at)

    if root.is_err()
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "the argument is not a place")

    match r.uses[root.must()]
        .local l =>
            one = r.locals[l.at]

            match one.kind
                .param =>
                    if one.mutable
                        return fail(code: "none", msg: "an `@` parameter is not borrowed")
                    return ok(root.must())
                .bind | .cell | .loop_var | .payload => return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a parameter")
        .top | .module | .builtin | .unresolved => return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a local")

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "unreached")

# The rule, at one call: for every parameter the callee marked `consumes`, if
# the argument in that position is rooted in a borrowed parameter, refuse.
#
# It runs on the NAMED-callee path only. A call through a function value carries
# no declaration, so there is no mark to read — which is a hole this says out
# loud rather than hides: `(function(Db) -> i64)` erases the word, as it erases
# every other thing a declaration knows.
function refuse_borrowed(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, s: source.Source, decl: ast.Decl, given: [ast.Arg])
    positions = consuming_positions(decl)

    if positions.len() == 0
        return
    callee = source.span_text(s, decl.name)

    for at in positions
        if at >= given.len()
            continue
        borrowed = borrowed_root(a, r, at: given[at].value)

        if !borrowed.is_err()
            span = a.exprs[given[at].value].span
            state.push_diagnostic(
                @c,
                refusal(borrowed: source.span_text(s, a.exprs[borrowed.must()].span), callee: callee, span: span)
            )

# The refusal. It names the parameter the caller borrowed, because that is the
# name to put an `@` on, and the consuming function, because that is what ends
# the value's life.
function refusal(borrowed: str, callee: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "consumed_borrowed_handle",
        message: "`" + callee + "` ends the life of what it is given, and `" + borrowed + "` was borrowed — this function promised not to change it",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "a parameter written without `@` is borrowed: the caller keeps the value and reads it again after the call, so a call that frees it leaves them holding an address C has taken back. Mark the parameter `@" + borrowed + "` and the call site `@" + borrowed + "` too, which says on both lines that the value does not survive"
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "this refuses the call, not the copy: a copy made BEFORE the call still holds the freed address, and nothing here sees that. design.md Part 8 wart 20 states that cost and names what would close it"
    )
    return d
